00:04:38.249 --> 00:04:43.069
A weather derivative pays on a measured index, which is how these contracts work, rather

00:04:43.069 --> 00:04:45.069
than on proof that one field failed.

00:04:45.629 --> 00:04:48.469
Cumulative average temperature adds up the season's average.

00:04:49.029 --> 00:04:52.289
Growing degree days add up how far temperature sits above a base.

00:04:52.889 --> 00:04:56.909
Product design basis risk is the miss between that index and actual yield.

00:04:57.549 --> 00:05:01.749
Geographical basis risk is the miss between the station and the contract and the farm.

00:05:01.749 --> 00:05:08.049
A 2019 paper on archive, on hedging crop yields against weather uncertainties, uses a machine

00:05:08.049 --> 00:05:13.089
learning ensemble to see which weather variables move maize yield, and reports that average

00:05:13.089 --> 00:05:14.249
temperature mattered most.

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:19.529
The temperature model it proposes is mean reverting, with a speed of reversion that can

00:05:19.529 --> 00:05:24.909
change over time, a seasonal level to revert toward, and local volatility that depends

00:05:24.909 --> 00:05:26.449
on the local average temperature.

00:05:26.449 --> 00:05:31.949
The authors stretch that into several locations at once, correlated with each other, and then

00:05:31.949 --> 00:05:37.869
write prices for futures, options on futures, and basket futures, on both cumulative average

00:05:37.869 --> 00:05:39.809
temperature and growing degree days.

00:05:40.329 --> 00:05:45.269
Follow their build without inventing a temperature or a premium, because the abstract gives neither.

00:05:45.969 --> 00:05:48.469
The ensemble picks average temperature as the driver.

00:05:49.069 --> 00:05:54.429
The path is pulled toward a seasonal mean, faster or slower as the speed changes, with

00:05:54.429 --> 00:05:59.369
volatility tied to the local average. A future's price is then the price of the cumulative

00:05:59.369 --> 00:06:04.909
average of that path, or of the degree days accumulated from it. A basket lets the buyer

00:06:04.909 --> 00:06:09.809
weight the stations that sit near the crop, and the paper says that choice reduces geographical

00:06:09.809 --> 00:06:16.229
basis risk. An option is only a right to that index exposure. The Ensemble step is their attempt

00:06:16.229 --> 00:06:22.309
to eliminate product design basis risk. A 2011 paper on archive on pricing weather derivatives

00:06:22.309 --> 00:06:26.229
for extreme events, models extremes as a max-stable process,

00:06:26.709 --> 00:06:29.609
drawing on spatial statistics and extreme value theory,

00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:32.969
then simulates payments across a general set of contracts.

00:06:33.569 --> 00:06:35.769
Those simulations keep the spatial dependence,

00:06:36.289 --> 00:06:39.449
so a bad outcome in one place is not treated as independent

00:06:39.449 --> 00:06:41.009
of a bad outcome next door.

00:06:41.669 --> 00:06:44.589
Catastrophe rate-making methods then supply risk loads

00:06:44.589 --> 00:06:47.229
and premiums for contracts that are renewal additive,

00:06:47.789 --> 00:06:50.029
so the load can be added across renewals.

00:06:50.029 --> 00:06:55.809
The 2019 paper says most small holder farmers and agricultural stakeholders will not pay

00:06:55.809 --> 00:07:00.309
weather-derivative prices because both kinds of basis risks sit in the models.

00:07:00.889 --> 00:07:05.749
The ensemble and the basket are the authors reply to that refusal, and the text does

00:07:05.749 --> 00:07:07.589
not say the refusal then disappears.

00:07:08.229 --> 00:07:14.029
The 2011 premium still depend on the max-stable description of the tail, and on those spatially

00:07:14.029 --> 00:07:18.129
linked simulations, so a bad description moves the risk load with it.

00:07:18.129 --> 00:07:23.549
The abstract names no outside critic of the fit, so a temperature price is a price of

00:07:23.549 --> 00:07:24.029
an index.

00:07:24.549 --> 00:07:29.289
Cumulative average temperature and growing degree days accumulate heat and cold differently.

00:07:29.809 --> 00:07:34.929
A basket is a weighted mix of places, a cut in geographical basis risk in that 2019

00:07:34.929 --> 00:07:37.669
account, not a promise the field matches.

00:07:38.369 --> 00:07:43.309
An extreme contract transfers the tail, and in the 2011 account the premium includes

00:07:43.309 --> 00:07:46.969
a catastrophe risk load, not only an expected payout.

00:07:46.969 --> 00:07:51.849
Knowing that tells you what risk the price is for, and what gap remains if the crop,

00:07:52.129 --> 00:07:54.049
the place, or the tail does not match.

